⏺ The webpage is built and the script checks out. Here's what you've got:

  rotating-ellipse.html (saved in your repo root)

  The spec, implemented exactly:
  - The ellipse is drawn from a semi-major axis a (constant) and a semi-minor 
    axis b that vacillates between two values b₁ and b₂
  - It rotates about its center at an adjustable speed, while b oscillates at 5×
    the rotation frequency — the rotation angle θ grows linearly and the minor
    axis follows b(θ) = (b₁+b₂)/2 + (b₂−b₁)/2 · cos 5θ, so b completes five full
    cycles per revolution

  Controls:
  - Axes — sliders for a (constant), b₁ (lower), b₂ (upper); the sliders clamp
    so b₁ ≤ b₂ ≤ a
  - Motion — rotation speed (2–120 rpm), Pause/Play, Reset
  - Display — toggle the rotating axis lines and the trace

  Live readouts — current rotation angle, current minor-axis value, and the 5:1
  ratio chip. The orange trace follows the tip of the minor axis: since its
  radius cycles 5 times per revolution, it inscribes a five-petal flower, which
  makes the 5:1 relationship visible at a glance. Dark/light themes are both
  handled, and it respects prefers-reduced-motion (starts paused).

  To see it:
  1. I can open it in your browser right now — say the word, or run ! open 
     rotating-ellipse.html yourself
  2. For a hosted preview link, I tried publishing it as an artifact, but that
     needs a claude.ai login (this session has ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN set
     instead). If you run /login and pick "Claude account with subscription", I
     can publish it
  3. Since it's a self-contained HTML file in your repo, committing and pushing
     makes it live at tthtlc.github.io/rotating-ellipse.html on your actual site
     — I can also wire it into the Jekyll nav if you'd like

  Want me to open it locally, or make it a proper page in the Jekyll site?

